>According to recent discussions in this group there are several
>Nethackers who think that the power of a wand of polymorph should
>be reduced somehow.

	Why?  Why not reduce the power of weapons while we're at
	it?  How about eliminating the ring of conflict?  Any character
	with one of those coupled with invisibility and regeneration
	is an almost guaranteed win...

	Speaking of invisibility, is the bug where monsters chuck
	weaponry (and aim breath weapons) at invisible characters with
	uncanny accuracy going to stick around?  I can understand them
	tracking the character down by sense of smell/hearing, but
	thrown weapons being more accurate than hitting?

>			In my best games I used the power of this wand to
>create hundreds of wishes (the development team has made this harder
>to do, but it's still possible).

	What you didn't mention is the amazing amount of patience that
	is required for this approach.  Personally I'd rather go onto
	a few more levels and find the items I'm looking for.

>I think that a wizard should never start a game with a wand of
>polymorph.  It is too tempting to quit any wizard that doesn't
>get one at the start of the game.

	It'd be nice if one could re-roll characters at the beginning
	of the game instead of exiting.  That way, the player spends
	some time getting a workable character, and there is not as
	much incentive to quit just because the equipment is not right.

>Players should be encouraged
>to play with whatever equipment their wizard starts with, and they
>should be discouraged from looking for just the right starting point
>in order to obtain such a large advantage.  (Starting with a wand of
>polymorph virtually ensures getting good equipment later in the game.)

	Hokayfine.  You can play a bozo with strength 7 and constituton
	10, equipped with a wand of opening, scrolls of garbage detection
	and potions of confusion.  I'll wait until I have a character
	that I has a chance.

	In fact, why not have a Peasant character class for the more 
	masochistic nethackers?  This would be an almost guaranteed abortion,
	with all stats under 10 and no useful equipment.  A win with one of 
	these would truly be a testament to enormous skill and luck.

>On the other hand, starting with a wand of polymorph does lead to some
>very interesting strategies for an experienced player.  To encourage
>creative play that is characterized by even fewer "brute force"
>techniques than are required now, I propose a new character class:

	It'll never fly.  A wand of polymorph isn't THAT much of
	an advantage.  A wand of wishing, on the other hand...

	I'd rather see a Merchant character class.  A sort of apprentice
	shopkeeper, if you will.  I don't know about beginning stats, but
	Wisdom, Intelligence and Charisma should be on the high side.

	The equipment would be about 10 items, chosen as they would appear
	in a shop.  True, if you start out with 10 things of food or 10
	scrolls/potions you're royally hosed, but otherwise it might turn
	out to be interesting.  Special ability might be automatic identify 
	for everything.  (type, not number of charges or level of enchantment.)

